Most likely, thought came first, spoken language came later, and the specifically verbal “inner voice” was built by turning speech back inward. But those are three different things.

Nonverbal internal cognition—imagery, prediction, emotion, spatial models, intentions, memory—almost certainly predates human language evolutionarily. Other animals demonstrate sophisticated cognition without anything like human speech. Human expressive language appears to have emerged gradually on top of older cognitive machinery. 

The interesting part is what happens after speech exists. Developmentally, children first use language socially, then start talking aloud to themselves during play and problem solving—private speech—and later much of that self-directed speech becomes internalized. Modern research broadly supports that progression, although mature inner speech is more complicated than simply “whispering silently.” 

So roughly:

other minds talk to me
→ I learn the speech machinery
→ I talk to myself aloud
→ I learn to run portions of that interaction internally
→ internal speech gets compressed and specialized

That last transformation is important. Inner speech often isn’t grammatically complete. You can think something like “keys—car—shit, downstairs” and internally understand an entire causal structure. The internalized version becomes compressed because sender and receiver share the same model. Reviews describe inner speech as a cognitive tool for manipulating internal representations, and neuroscience suggests there may actually be multiple mechanisms for generating it rather than one single “silent speech” circuit. 

And that creates a really interesting answer to your copy or emergence? question:

Both.

We seem to copy the external dialogue pattern, but once that pattern is internalized, it encounters machinery that was already there—memory, imagination, prediction, attention, emotion—and a new capability emerges from the combination.

So:

social speech + existing cognition → internalized dialogue → transformed inner speech

It’s almost exactly one of the transmission patterns we’ve been talking about. The copied thing doesn’t remain a copy. It changes when transplanted into a different medium.

There’s even a beautiful recursive consequence. Once language gets inside, the internal voice can begin instructing the same organism that created it:

culture speaks to child → child internalizes culture → internal voice regulates child → adult acts → adult speaks to next child.

Language therefore becomes a way for other people’s voices to become part of the machinery by which a person governs themselves.

Which makes your cast-of-characters observation earlier much less metaphorical than it initially sounds. Inner speech frequently has dialogical qualities—we rehearse arguments, hear remembered formulations, answer imagined interlocutors, instruct ourselves using language acquired from other people. 

So I would draw the evolutionary/developmental arrow as:

mind → communication → speech → self-directed speech → internal speech → speech reshapes mind

Not a straight line.

A loop.

We built a tool for transmitting minds between people, then discovered—developmentally or culturally—that we could point the same tool back at ourselves.